WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Speaker 1: Let's go to our old&nbsp;
friend Brody from Dallas. Brody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.440
please don't torpedo today's session of calls.
Speaker 6: I'm going to do my best. David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.520
how's it going, man? Going well. Awesome. Hey,&nbsp;
so we're fighting the good fight out here in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.600
Texas. We recently had a huge fiasco in the&nbsp;
primary elections. We had a huge disparity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.320
We had about eight hundred and fifty Democratic&nbsp;
primary voters had their mail in ballots rejected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:35.440
and less than 10 Republican primary voters had&nbsp;
their ballots rejected. One of the things we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.080
struggled with here is getting help from D.C.&nbsp;
I know that there is talk about Joe mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.360
possibly carving out some things for different&nbsp;
voting rights legislation. What I wanted to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:54.320
is what do you see as the most viable path for&nbsp;
states like Texas to have protection from D.C.?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:00.400
Or do you believe that it's just going to have&nbsp;
to come from all the state legislatures? And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.880
a follow up, what would you like to see happen?
Speaker 1: So the I don't I don't know exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.000
what you're referring to in Texas. So without&nbsp;
knowing the dynamics of it, it's very hard to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.800
specifically what could address that? So&nbsp;
just with that, as a kind of disclaimer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:25.120
I would love to see a new Voting Rights Act that&nbsp;
restricts the things that states can do and allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.640
for federal oversight. I think many states,&nbsp;
you know, they with a couple of years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.200
states were sort of released from their&nbsp;
requirements to pre-clear changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.160
I think we need to be adding more requirements&nbsp;
in terms of changes that states want to make.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.600
That would be the ideal thing. I don't know that&nbsp;
that's going to happen. And so of course, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.680
be great if states did legislation on voting&nbsp;
rights within their own states. But the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.000
is, Republican controlled states don't really have&nbsp;
any interest in doing that. So it's a very tough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.000
situation. I don't know what the answer is.
Speaker 6: And then as a personal question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.000
would you be willing like, let's say,&nbsp;
would you compromise at all if, let's say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.840
Republicans in the GOP in the Senate particularly&nbsp;
wanted to enact something which you as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:22.480
progressive and just you personally be willing to&nbsp;
acquiesce or agree to, maybe including voter I.D.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.720
being required as a part of that so long&nbsp;
as it doesn't disenfranchize people?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:33.040
Or do you believe that voting ID is something&nbsp;
that should be non-negotiable from the Democrat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.640
and the progressive side of things?
Speaker 1: So two part answer and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.720
are really, you know, you know, these are big&nbsp;
topics, right? So there's a lot of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:48.960
considerations. I am very skeptical of voter&nbsp;
ID because it is a transparently bad faith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.640
way for Republicans to disenfranchize people. So&nbsp;
that's why I have such a problem with voter ID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.280
as proposed and implemented. That&nbsp;
being said, if there was a voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.720
rights package that did significant&nbsp;
good things and codified them into law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:13.760
I'd be open to negotiating on voter ID, provided&nbsp;
I was satisfied that there really is, you know, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:22.000
can't cost money to get the documents, you need&nbsp;
to get the voter ID. It can't require rural poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.080
people to drive to the state capital in order to&nbsp;
get the documents they need to get the voter I.D.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.520
like if I was really satisfied that all of those&nbsp;
things were were dealt with. I'd be willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.960
negotiate on voter ID as part of a bigger voting&nbsp;
rights package. The problem is, in practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.520
we know it's just a bad faith way to purge people&nbsp;
from voter rolls and prevent people from voting.&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.280
Speaker 6: Absolutely. Well, hey, I'm glad to have&nbsp;
talked with you, and I hope I lived up at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.520
moderately or mildly to the expectations.&nbsp;
And it's good to speak to you again and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.040
please encourage all your voters like you&nbsp;
always do to register and go out and vote.&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.560
Speaker 1: Everyone must vote. Brody, thank you&nbsp;
so much for the call. Let's take a quick break,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.960
but we're going right back to the phones in&nbsp;
a moment. So if you still want to talk to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.560
after hearing all of these calls, if you&nbsp;
still want to talk to me, just hang on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.968
All right, let's go back to the phones where&nbsp;
so many people want to chat. Let's start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:55.840
Jordan from Minnesota. Jordan, you're on the&nbsp;
air. Jordan, from Minnesota, you're on the air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:31.680
Not a great start. Not a great start&nbsp;
here, I guess. No, Jordan from Minnesota.

